  "query_preview": "..\nAs of 239 PM EDT Wednesday...\n\nKEY MESSAGE 1: While conditions are not expected to reach Red Flag \nCriteria in the upcoming future, gusty conditions and lower relative \nhumidity values will cause increased fire weather concerns due to \nplentiful dry leaves on the ground serving as fuel. Winds gusts \ntomorrow and Friday are generally expected to be in the 15 to 25 mph \nrange, with some gusts up to 30 mph possible. The highest gusts are \nexpected in the St. Lawrence Valley and northern slopes of the \nAdirondacks where gusts up to 35 mph are possible tomorrow. Relative \nhumidity values will generally be in the 40-55 percent range \ntomorrow. Relative humidity values are expected in that range again \non Friday for most places, but localized drops into the 30-40 \npercent range are possible in the Lower Connecticut River Valley. \nHeading into the weekend, winds will mostly remain under 15 mph. \nHowever, relative humidity values should drop into the 30-45 percent \nrange for most places, with a localized drop into upper 20s possible \nin the Lower Connecticut Valley. A few showers are possible tomorrow \nevening and overnight, but a wetting rain is not expected anywhere.\n\nKEY MESSAGE 2: After a warm day tomorrow, a cold front comes through \ntomorrow night and into early Friday. A few showers are possible \nacross northern New York and the higher elevations of northern \nVermont but with dry air aloft and weak forcing, they will be brief \nand only lead to a couple hundreths of an inch of rain locally. The \nhighest amounts are expected in the northern Greens and the \nAdirondacks. Strong cold air advection will occur on Friday with \nclouds in the typical upslope areas. Surface high pressure builds \noverhead into the weekend, leading to mostly sunny skies and \nmoderating temperatures. The Halloween forecast looks great with \nclear skies, light winds and temperatures quickly dropping from the \n50s into the 30s during the evening. However, despite clear skies, \nany added ambiance from the moon will mostly be missing as it was \nfull a couple days ago and will rise around 10:45 Halloween evening.\n\nKEY MESSAGE 3: While no hazardous weather is expected, there is some \ndisagreement amongst ensemble guidance on the upper level pattern \nearly next week. The consensus idea amongst non-GEFS guidance \nstrongly favors a more amplified ridge to our west maintaining \nnorthwest flow over our region and deflecting most precipitation \nwell to our west. In this most likely scenario, low level \ntemperatures remain above normal with near surface winds primarily \nout of the south and chances of rain are very low. \n\nWhile certainly not indicating any significant weather, the majority \nof GEFS membership in contrast suggests some weak troughing that \nallows a shortwave to push showers in from the west. As such, this \nless likely scenario brings rainfall chances closer to 60% to 75% in \nthe Monday afternoon through Tuesday timeframe, and for amounts \nexceeding 0.25\" mainly 20-30%, or slightly higher in the \nAdirondacks. \n\nFinally, there are indications that a weak surface cold front \nsimilar to ones we've seen in recent weeks could slip in from the \nnorth. It would bring chances for very light rain and a cloudier \nperiod with reinforcement of seasonably cool air. This complicates \nthe temperature forecast for Tuesday, when the high temperature \nforecast is relatively uncertain compared to Monday (about twice the \nmodel spread).",
